[38;5;239m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mgood question — I haven't thought that through. What would you suggest? No strong preference from me.[39m

[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m You picked time-based reminders and said you have no strong preference beyond that — so I'll make the calls from here
  and show you the result, flagging assumptions as I go. But there's one constraint I have to put in front of you before
  I design anything, because it can invalidate the whole feature.

  [1mA purely client-side reminder can only fire while a tab is open.[0m That's a browser limitation, not a design choice:

  - Tab open and focused: a timer plus the Notification API works fine.
  - Tab open but backgrounded: timers get heavily throttled — a reminder can land minutes late.
  - Tab closed: nothing fires. The API that would have allowed scheduled notifications without a server (Notification
    Triggers) never shipped past an origin trial. Web Push does work when the app is closed, but it requires a server to
    send the push.

  The uncomfortable implication: the point of a reminder is usually to reach you when you [3maren't[0m looking at the app, and
  that's exactly the case a static page can't serve. I don't want to hand you a reminder system that only reminds you
  of things you can already see on screen.

  So the real fork is whether "reaches me when the app is closed" is a requirement or a nice-to-have:

  - [1mIn-app only, accept the limitation.[0m Reminders surface when the app is open — overdue badges, a due-soon list, an OS
    notification if the tab happens to be open. Honest, small, entirely client-side, no infrastructure. Cost: it is a
    glorified due-date view, and you should judge it as one.
  - [1mAdd a server for real push.[0m Reminders actually reach the user. Cost: a backend, a datastore, accounts (push
    subscriptions have to belong to someone), VAPID keys, and a deploy story — the project stops being a static page.
    Not reversible in the sense that the task storage design changes underneath.

  My recommendation is [1min-app only for v1[0m, with the reminder-evaluation logic kept in one module that takes "now" as an
  input and returns what's due. That's the piece a server would later need too, so choosing this doesn't force a rewrite

